{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2280 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "30e3d3ebecb6fee6c6f1b8da5dc8eb083a6f8bf5debf3af04b0c11009971294c" :edb/db-before-t 2279 :edb/db-after-t 2280 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535592 50 #inst "2026-10-09T03:10:20.770Z" 13194139535592 true] [17592186179575 1020 #edb/ref 17592186356655 13194139535592 true] [17592186179576 1020 #edb/ref 17592186356662 13194139535592 true] [17592186179577 1020 #edb/ref 17592186356669 13194139535592 true] [17592186179577 1020 #edb/ref 17592186356676 13194139535592 true] [17592186179966 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535592 true] [17592186179966 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068094/assets/b37c10064003374c146c58bba99ff55304529ee66c9fb38c44246ccc90e8f402.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i+{y}^{2}\\,j$ and the square $C$ with side length $4$ centered at $O,$ shown above. If the flux of $F$ across the side ${C}_{1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" 13194139535592 true] [17592186179966 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3715/Source/Images/q-126850.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x, y) = x^{2}i + y^{2}j$ and the square $C$ with side length $4$ centered at $O$, shown above. If the flux of $F$ across the side $C_{1}$ is given by\n\n$∫_{C_{1}}AAAAAAAds$,\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" 13194139535592 false] [17592186179966 1145 "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nIn our case, we need to find an expression for the flux across only the part ${C}_{1}$ of the closed curve $C.$ Since this side is perpendicular to the $x$-axis and we require an outward-pointing unit normal vector, we must have $n=i.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068094/assets/c4c4f2f3d5b6188ac5a6ec41bb96fc0c7132cc39308cebfa16ee759eabac2c75.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}({x}^{2}\\,i+{y}^{2}\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is ${x}^{2}.$" 13194139535592 true] [17592186356655 1017 #edb/ref 17592186356656 13194139535592 true] [17592186356655 1089 "q-127053" 13194139535592 true] [17592186356655 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535592 true] [17592186356655 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068094/assets/4d9663a0a40f3c3860cce0712e9e9aab41d55251ea15fca33ec663515af57d10.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y)={x}^{3}y\\,i-x{y}^{2}\\,j$ and the rectangle $C,$ shown above. If the flux of $F$ across the side ${C}_{3}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" 13194139535592 true] [17592186356655 1113 #uuid "399b0f43-066e-5c14-a2a9-0d532f977caf" 13194139535592 true] [17592186356655 1145 "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nIn our case, we need to find an expression for the flux across only the part ${C}_{3}$ of the closed curve $C.$ Since this side is perpendicular to the $x$-axis and we require an outward-pointing unit normal vector, we must have $n=-i.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068094/assets/c6dd493e02633854184be1be0f38246e1e9dce2949f9cc1aa93b943c91d1e44c.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}({x}^{3}y\\,i-x{y}^{2}\\,j)\\cdot (-i)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}-{x}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}s\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $-{x}^{3}y.$" 13194139535592 true] [17592186356656 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356657 13194139535592 true] [17592186356656 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356658 13194139535592 true] [17592186356656 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356659 13194139535592 true] [17592186356656 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356660 13194139535592 true] [17592186356656 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356661 13194139535592 true] [17592186356656 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535592 true] [17592186356656 1041 "selection" 13194139535592 true] [17592186356656 1104 #edb/ref 17592186356657 13194139535592 true] [17592186356656 1110 #uuid "97a95df4-4595-5700-aa15-ef9aa524562f" 13194139535592 true] [17592186356657 1034 "-{x}^{3}y" 13194139535592 true] [17592186356657 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535592 true] [17592186356657 1111 #uuid "70ce25c1-89c9-5ab4-a3dd-d1500999bfbb" 13194139535592 true] [17592186356658 1034 "-{x}^{3}{y}^{2}" 13194139535592 true] [17592186356658 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535592 true] [17592186356658 1111 #uuid "c6ea3d1b-b8d0-5748-841f-b094efde515c" 13194139535592 true] [17592186356659 1034 "-x{y}^{2}" 13194139535592 true] [17592186356659 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535592 true] [17592186356659 1111 #uuid "08c9acbf-86ef-524a-8651-e1905ebad3e1" 13194139535592 true] [17592186356660 1034 "x{y}^{2}" 13194139535592 true] [17592186356660 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535592 true] [17592186356660 1111 #uuid "87df86a4-54e8-508d-aab8-0bdec254c6ae" 13194139535592 true] [17592186356661 1034 "{x}^{3}y" 13194139535592 true] [17592186356661 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535592 true] [17592186356661 1111 #uuid "e617e31a-9325-59cb-b29a-bc8f1ae2b172" 13194139535592 true] [17592186356662 1017 #edb/ref 17592186356663 13194139535592 true] [17592186356662 1089 "q-226126" 13194139535592 true] [17592186356662 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535592 true] [17592186356662 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068094/assets/6f827dac7ecaf3a659245d1178aa39a519ae3a40339c917004247f12554c871a.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=2x\\,i+2y\\,j$ and a square $C$ with side length $8$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across all four sides of the square.*" 13194139535592 true] [17592186356662 1113 #uuid "580feb93-1f7c-52cc-bad0-610745640670" 13194139535592 true] [17592186356662 1145 "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068094/assets/0b753318ebad9270c3fc8788d0e3949ffb29a800eb3f8dd6fb67cb271be68033.png)\n\nFirst, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${n}_{1}=i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(2x\\,i+2y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=4\\,i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-4,4].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & =2{\\int }_{-4}^{4}4\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{-4}^{4}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8(4-(-4)) \\\\  & =8\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =64.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integrals over the sides ${C}_{2},{C}_{3},$ and ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=4\\cdot 64=256.\n$$" 13194139535592 true] [17592186356663 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356664 13194139535592 true] [17592186356663 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356665 13194139535592 true] [17592186356663 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356666 13194139535592 true] [17592186356663 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356667 13194139535592 true] [17592186356663 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356668 13194139535592 true] [17592186356663 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535592 true] [17592186356663 1041 "selection" 13194139535592 true] [17592186356663 1104 #edb/ref 17592186356668 13194139535592 true] [17592186356663 1110 #uuid "b8e8ed30-9e50-5012-b659-ccdafefd0f09" 13194139535592 true] [17592186356664 1034 "64" 13194139535592 true] [17592186356664 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535592 true] [17592186356664 1111 #uuid "af200a91-387e-5092-8ed6-16245b09067d" 13194139535592 true] [17592186356665 1034 "288" 13194139535592 true] [17592186356665 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535592 true] [17592186356665 1111 #uuid "7ef4c183-cd0a-51fa-bc95-7019860c3c35" 13194139535592 true] [17592186356666 1034 "128" 13194139535592 true] [17592186356666 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535592 true] [17592186356666 1111 #uuid "3604b747-edfc-5408-aa72-5f0d2fe68c0a" 13194139535592 true] [17592186356667 1034 "72" 13194139535592 true] [17592186356667 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535592 true] [17592186356667 1111 #uuid "1585c90e-2dab-5e64-aec7-269ec7ef3c64" 13194139535592 true] [17592186356668 1034 "256" 13194139535592 true] [17592186356668 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535592 true] [17592186356668 1111 #uuid "d464ee90-50ea-5bd1-accb-64d217108f4e" 13194139535592 true] [17592186356669 1017 #edb/ref 17592186356670 13194139535592 true] [17592186356669 1089 "q-226133" 13194139535592 true] [17592186356669 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535592 true] [17592186356669 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068094/assets/2dcfb8778adf7e9a05ff1dff886d0d8290427ed2ae910b46db4fe9aeb36170eb.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=x\\,i+3y\\,j$ and a square $C$ with side length $6$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across the vertical sides, and similarly for the horizontal sides.*" 13194139535592 true] [17592186356669 1113 #uuid "189fc286-2334-55b5-beea-f2158d1888cd" 13194139535592 true] [17592186356669 1145 "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068094/assets/f283fe675e0a9ef736947e404f03ab5afb90a4209ba1a6949d18ac6d3fb20048.png)\n\nWe compute this integral in two stages. \n\n- First, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${n}_{1}=i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(x\\,i+3y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=3\\,i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-3,3].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{-3}^{3}3\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{3}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s=18.\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider the side ${C}_{2}.$ We have that ${n}_{2}=j,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}(x\\,i+3y\\,j)\\cdot j\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=t\\,i+3\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=i,\\,t\\in [-3,3].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s & =3{\\int }_{-3}^{3}3\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =9\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =54.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s=54.\n$$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=2\\cdot 18+2\\cdot 54=144.\n$$" 13194139535592 true] [17592186356670 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356671 13194139535592 true] [17592186356670 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356672 13194139535592 true] [17592186356670 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356673 13194139535592 true] [17592186356670 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356674 13194139535592 true] [17592186356670 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356675 13194139535592 true] [17592186356670 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535592 true] [17592186356670 1041 "selection" 13194139535592 true] [17592186356670 1104 #edb/ref 17592186356675 13194139535592 true] [17592186356670 1110 #uuid "7b7971a2-f9e4-5fbf-9193-7fdfb754b51c" 13194139535592 true] [17592186356671 1034 "180" 13194139535592 true] [17592186356671 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535592 true] [17592186356671 1111 #uuid "d4b764d4-8ff5-5cd1-8956-3376bbe210ff" 13194139535592 true] [17592186356672 1034 "164" 13194139535592 true] [17592186356672 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535592 true] [17592186356672 1111 #uuid "55878f72-f35c-51ad-8a28-3eaca9a85e46" 13194139535592 true] [17592186356673 1034 "156" 13194139535592 true] [17592186356673 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535592 true] [17592186356673 1111 #uuid "e9c60e56-bee5-5240-bd3d-20144d704645" 13194139535592 true] [17592186356674 1034 "176" 13194139535592 true] [17592186356674 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535592 true] [17592186356674 1111 #uuid "f975eadf-2095-52f6-9e7b-b67af944c01c" 13194139535592 true] [17592186356675 1034 "144" 13194139535592 true] [17592186356675 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535592 true] [17592186356675 1111 #uuid "a904007b-d324-56ec-9feb-6d521fe9c00c" 13194139535592 true] [17592186356676 1017 #edb/ref 17592186356677 13194139535592 true] [17592186356676 1089 "q-226150" 13194139535592 true] [17592186356676 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535592 true] [17592186356676 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068094/assets/80cc5e165f78bc1fc8cc7f83644fd00ca4a8f4e05e69d97b654bb2f1450d9b99.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=-x\\,i+3y\\,j$ and a rectangle $C$ with base $6$ and height $4$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across the vertical sides, and similarly for the horizontal sides.*" 13194139535592 true] [17592186356676 1113 #uuid "51d2761b-06a2-53c6-8491-c0e447a59ac2" 13194139535592 true] [17592186356676 1145 "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068094/assets/511bae61d710c3362bb18618717b3d44ef5dcedece5f669e0b5237b7b524a054.png)\n\nWe compute this integral in two stages. \n\n- First, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${n}_{1}=i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(-x\\,i+3y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=3i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-2,2].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & =-{\\int }_{-2}^{2}3\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{3}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s=-12.\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider the side ${C}_{2}.$ We have that ${n}_{2}=j,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}(-x\\,i+3y\\,j)\\cdot j\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=t\\,i+2\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=i,\\,t\\in [-3,3].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s & =3{\\int }_{-3}^{3}2\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s=36.\n$$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=2\\cdot (-12)+2\\cdot 36=48.\n$$" 13194139535592 true] [17592186356677 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356678 13194139535592 true] [17592186356677 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356679 13194139535592 true] [17592186356677 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356680 13194139535592 true] [17592186356677 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356681 13194139535592 true] [17592186356677 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356682 13194139535592 true] [17592186356677 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535592 true] [17592186356677 1041 "selection" 13194139535592 true] [17592186356677 1104 #edb/ref 17592186356681 13194139535592 true] [17592186356677 1110 #uuid "300c6f51-3106-536a-89c6-b3f0ee473aa4" 13194139535592 true] [17592186356678 1034 "36" 13194139535592 true] [17592186356678 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535592 true] [17592186356678 1111 #uuid "585b82ef-e6e9-5f60-8a82-3e64d405f965" 13194139535592 true] [17592186356679 1034 "42" 13194139535592 true] [17592186356679 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535592 true] [17592186356679 1111 #uuid "ab7659e8-64d7-5af0-b2e6-388983dbc232" 13194139535592 true] [17592186356680 1034 "32" 13194139535592 true] [17592186356680 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535592 true] [17592186356680 1111 #uuid "21cc8b7c-0217-50cb-aaf9-f6b4181d69fc" 13194139535592 true] [17592186356681 1034 "48" 13194139535592 true] [17592186356681 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535592 true] [17592186356681 1111 #uuid "3e5f6b06-b6bf-5ff0-94d1-46b5ad229d8d" 13194139535592 true] [17592186356682 1034 "54" 13194139535592 true] [17592186356682 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535592 true] [17592186356682 